00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:56.330
Can someone remind me if I have
a vector u here

00:03:57.080 --> 00:04:03.040
and a vector v here, what is the
orthogonal projection of u onto

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:03.660
v?

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:06.660
What does that mean?

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:11.360
What was the orthogonal
projection of u onto v?

00:04:12.740 --> 00:04:14.520
What was that?

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:15.400
Alex.

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:19.840
It's making a vector in the same
direction as v that most closely

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:21.340
matches u.

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:23.840
That was the intuitive way I
wanted you to think about this.

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:27.340
The orthogonal projection of u
onto v

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:31.700
is the vector in the direction
of v

00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:35.740
that is as close to u as
possible.

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:37.380
How was that measured?

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:42.000
The way we measured how close
our approximation was was by

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:46.040
subtracting u from the scalar
multiple of v we found.

00:04:48.140 --> 00:04:51.180
So these are all scalar
multiples of v.

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:54.200
We're looking for the scalar
multiple of v that is closest to

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:55.200
u.

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:02.880
And that vector we gave the name
p,

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:05.880
which is the orthogonal
projection of u onto v.

00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:10.140
And we also called it k star
times v.

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:12.390
What was K-star?

00:05:15.940 --> 00:05:17.620
It's a scalar.

00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:21.660
It told us how much of v we
should take to make this

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:23.380
orthogonal projection.
What was that amount? Do you

00:05:23.380 --> 00:05:24.380
