Half of v is going to look
something like that.

00:28:40.460 --> 00:28:44.960
All of those scalar multiples of
v live in this line.

00:28:46.640 --> 00:28:49.460
And similarly, if I pick any
point on this line,

00:28:49.460 --> 00:28:51.860
I could think of what scalar
multiple I would have to

00:28:51.860 --> 00:28:55.440
multiply v by to get there.

00:28:55.440 --> 00:29:00.400
Okay, and so this line

00:29:00.400 --> 00:29:08.600
is

00:29:08.600 --> 00:29:11.600
the span of v,

00:29:14.180 --> 00:29:18.700
okay,

00:29:18.920 --> 00:29:22.680
and if I want to write this in
set notation,

00:29:22.680 --> 00:29:27.640
I would write it as the set of
all vectors of the form a times

00:29:27.640 --> 00:29:28.220
1,

00:29:28.220 --> 00:29:33.470
2, where a is a real number.

00:29:34.340 --> 00:29:36.100
Doesn't matter what letter I use
here.

00:29:36.100 --> 00:29:37.780
If you look in the notes,

00:29:37.780 --> 00:29:41.530
sometimes instead of a, we'll
write t.

00:29:41.580 --> 00:29:46.060
t is a common choice for a
scalar or placeholder in this

00:29:46.060 --> 00:29:47.060
setting.

00:29:48.660 --> 00:29:49.660
OK?

00:29:50.660 --> 00:29:51.660
Now,

00:29:52.540 --> 00:29:56.290
what is another equation for
this line?

00:30:08.360 --> 00:30:12.580
What if I just want a regular y
equals mx plus b type equation

00:30:12.580 --> 00:30:14.830
for this line?

00:30:14.840 --> 00:30:16.880
How could I describe it?

00:30:16.880 --> 00:30:19.080
Not that we need one.
This is a perfectly valid way of

00:30:19.080 --> 00:30:20.500
describing a line,

00:30:20.500 --> 00:30:23.800
saying it's a set of all vectors
whose endpoints live on the

00:30:23.800 --> 00:30:24.800
line.

00:30:24.820 --> 00:30:28.120
And that set comes from
multiplying 1, 2 by all possible

00:30:28.120 --> 00:30:29.540
real numbers.

00:30:29.540 --> 00:30:30.120
Yeah?

00:30:30.120 --> 00:30:31.680
y equals 2x.

00:30:31.680 --> 00:30:33.930
y equals 2x.

00:30:34.820 --> 00:30:37.120
How did you think of that?

00:30:37.120 --> 00:30:38.840
I just looked at v,

00:30:38.840 --> 00:30:41.400
and then I did rise over run.
Good.

00:30:41.400 --> 00:30:43.380
Yeah, that's all it is.

00:30:43.380 --> 00:30:46.420
If you take a look at the vector
that spans the line, v,

00:30:46.420 --> 00:30:49.040
it consists of all of the
vectors where the y component is

00:30:49.040 --> 00:30:51.760
double the x component.

00:30:51.760 --> 00:30:54.320
If you multiply v by any number,
17,

00:30:54.320 --> 00:30:55.680
you'll get 17 and 34,

00:30:55.680 --> 00:31:00.180
which means y is still 2 times
x.

00:31:00.180 --> 00:31:05.980
So y equals 2x is the same line

00:31:05.980 --> 00:31:09.730
that the set builder notation
I've just highlighted describes.

00:31:10.840 --> 00:31:16.090
So we have a new way of
describing a line.

00:31:18.120 --> 00:31:21.080
One thing I want to point out
here

00:31:21.080 --> 00:31:27.000
is that the line passes through

00:31:27.000 --> 00:31:30.140
0, 0.

00:31:31.220 --> 00:31:32.500
Why is that?

00:31:32.500 --> 00:31:38.500
Like, why do I know for sure it
passes through 0, 0?

00:31:45.100 --> 00:31:46.700
I guess at 0,

00:31:46.700 --> 00:31:49.120
would you want the scalar
constants?

00:31:49.120 --> 00:31:49.760
Exactly.

00:31:49.760 --> 00:31:52.300
What if I multiply v by 0?

00:31:52.300 --> 00:31:54.540
That should give me the vector
0, 0.

00:31:54.540 --> 00:31:57.620
And since this line is supposed
to contain every scalar multiple

00:31:57.620 --> 00:31:59.080
of v,

00:31:59.080 --> 00:32:02.860
it should also contain 0, 0.

00:32:02.860 --> 00:32:06.640
Note that we only have one
building block vector, v, here.

00:32:06.640 --> 00:32:08.240
So the set that I've
highlighted,

00:32:08.240 --> 00:32:10.460
a times 1, 2, where a is a real
number,

00:32:10.460 --> 00:32:13.340
is the set of all linear
combinations of v.

00:32:13.340 --> 00:32:14.960
It's just a little strange to
think of it as a linear

00:32:14.960 --> 00:32:16.640
combination because there's only
one vector.

00:32:16.640 --> 00:32:17.620
You only have one building
block.

00:32:17.620 --> 00:32:19.740
The only thing you can do is
change how much of it you're

00:32:19.740 --> 00:32:20.860
taking.
There's nothing to add to.

00:32:21.820 --> 00:32:23.560
I have a misunderstanding of
this concept,

00:32:23.560 --> 00:32:26.220
but how does that work if the
vector,

00:32:26.220 --> 00:32:30.760
like I thought you said early in
the semester, that the placement

00:32:30.760 --> 00:32:33.520
of the vector doesn't matter.

00:32:33.520 --> 00:32:35.500
Where it physically is on the
graph.

00:32:35.500 --> 00:32:36.260
That is true.

00:32:36.260 --> 00:32:39.280
But suppose we consider 1, 2,

00:32:39.280 --> 00:32:43.260
and all of its scalar multiples,
which are like 2,

00:32:43.260 --> 00:32:44.260
4,

00:32:45.000 --> 00:32:46.500
3, 6,

00:32:46.780 --> 00:32:48.280
0, 0.

00:32:49.380 --> 00:32:52.080
There's only one line that
contains all of those vectors

00:32:52.080 --> 00:32:55.060
and it's the one that goes
through the origin.

00:32:55.060 --> 00:33:00.360
So, what I think will sort of
address your concern is how

00:33:00.360 --> 00:33:04.860
would we describe a line that
doesn't go through the origin?

00:33:06.100 --> 00:33:06.600
Okay,

00:33:06.600 --> 00:33:08.120
because this one,

00:33:08.120 --> 00:33:10.440
if you take for a moment that,

00:33:10.440 --> 00:33:12.240
you know, take at face value
what I'm saying,

00:33:12.240 --> 00:33:15.260
the span of a vector has to
contain 0, 0.

00:33:15.260 --> 00:33:18.660
What about lines that don't pass
through 0, 0?

00:33:18.660 --> 00:33:20.040
Like this one.

00:33:20.040 --> 00:33:22.560
Let's just pick two points here.

00:33:22.560 --> 00:33:27.960
0 comma 6 and 3 comma 12.

00:33:29.480 --> 00:33:29.940
OK?

00:33:29.940 --> 00:33:31.440
That line.

00:33:37.800 --> 00:33:41.550
This doesn't contain the origin,

00:33:44.020 --> 00:33:46.270
so it's not

00:33:50.160 --> 00:33:54.660
The span of any one vector.

00:33:57.980 --> 00:34:01.680
But I can still kind of describe
it

00:34:01.680 --> 00:34:04.360
like the span of a vector.

00:34:04.360 --> 00:34:06.360
It's just offset a little.

00:34:06.360 --> 00:34:08.020
You see what I'm getting at?

00:34:08.020 --> 00:34:11.800
Pretend this whole line, if I
dragged it down six units,

00:34:11.800 --> 00:34:13.580
and it did pass through the
origin.

00:34:13.580 --> 00:34:16.820
And then I could describe it as
the span of a vector.

00:34:16.820 --> 00:34:21.320
What vector could I describe it
as the span of?

00:34:24.740 --> 00:34:26.440
If it did go through the origin,

00:34:26.440 --> 00:34:28.500
I pulled it down,

00:34:28.500 --> 00:34:30.780
there is a vector, there is
plenty of vectors.

00:34:30.780 --> 00:34:32.040
The answer is not unique,

00:34:32.040 --> 00:34:34.180
that I could describe this line
as a span of.

00:34:34.180 --> 00:34:34.720
Yeah?

00:34:34.720 --> 00:34:36.220
3, 6.

00:34:36.300 --> 00:34:38.120
3, 6.
How'd you think of that? When

00:34:38.120 --> 00:34:42.640
you drag it down by 6, I just
subtract it 6 from 12.

00:34:42.640 --> 00:34:43.640
Good.

00:34:44.700 --> 00:34:46.600
Another way to think about it,
if you don't even want to drag

00:34:46.600 --> 00:34:49.220
it down, is think about the
change in y and change in x.

00:34:49.220 --> 00:34:50.900
What's the change in y here? 6.

00:34:50.900 --> 00:34:52.440
What's the change in x? 3.

00:34:52.440 --> 00:34:56.220
So it seems like this line has a
direction that moves 3 to the

00:34:56.220 --> 00:34:58.640
right and 6 up.

00:34:58.640 --> 00:35:00.660
So it's the same vector as the
other one, though, right?

00:35:00.660 --> 00:35:01.960
Because they're both just 2,

00:35:01.960 --> 00:35:05.000
like the slope is 3.
Yeah, you can also call it 1, 2.

00:35:05.000 --> 00:35:08.000
OK, so it is the same vector,
it's just? The direction vector

00:35:08.000 --> 00:35:11.440
is the same, but this is not the
same line.

00:35:11.440 --> 00:35:13.480
So the question is, how do you
describe this line? And this is

00:35:13.480 --> 00:35:16.200
a good segue, so I'm glad you
asked the question.

00:35:16.200 --> 00:35:21.460
I could describe this line like
this.

00:35:22.260 --> 00:35:24.780
It's the set of all scalar
multiples of 1,

00:35:24.780 --> 00:35:25.640
2,

00:35:25.640 --> 00:35:27.890
or 3, 6,

00:35:29.720 --> 00:35:33.470
but there's something added to
all of those.

00:35:35.580 --> 00:35:38.580
There's a plus something.

00:35:40.080 --> 00:35:40.360
0,

00:35:40.360 --> 00:35:41.860
6, sure.

00:35:44.460 --> 00:35:46.200
But that's not the only thing I
could put there.

00:35:46.200 --> 00:35:48.840
I could put any point on the
line

00:35:48.840 --> 00:35:52.340
that just tells me, you know, a
fixed place on the line and then

00:35:52.340 --> 00:35:53.340
says,

00:35:53.840 --> 00:35:54.140
okay,

00:35:54.140 --> 00:35:55.780
once you move to that fixed
point,

00:35:55.780 --> 00:35:58.960
go as far along this direction
vector as you would like.

00:35:58.960 --> 00:36:00.800
So I could put 0, 6 there, it
seems like the most natural

00:36:00.800 --> 00:36:01.800
choice.

00:36:02.020 --> 00:36:05.620
Nothing's stopping me from
putting 3, 12 there either.

00:36:06.620 --> 00:36:09.480
Because if I let a be equal to
negative 3, well, that would

00:36:09.480 --> 00:36:14.730
slide me back down to 0, 6.

00:36:18.800 --> 00:36:23.300
So this is a way I would
describe this line

00:36:23.300 --> 00:36:25.420
as involving the span of a
vector.

00:36:25.420 --> 00:36:27.120
But it's not the span of the
vector.

00:36:27.120 --> 00:36:30.460
It's sort of shifted because it
does not contain the origin.

00:36:30.460 --> 00:36:33.460
It's a good question.

00:36:39.240 --> 00:36:41.640
And actually, just to clarify
that there's several ways you

00:36:41.640 --> 00:36:43.890
can do this,

00:36:44.340 --> 00:36:49.640
let me also write as an example,
0, 6 plus A times, you know,

00:36:50.120 --> 00:36:51.620
3, 12.

00:37:00.700 --> 00:37:06.520
And I was going to save this
definition

00:37:06.520 --> 00:37:10.480
for a little later, but this set
builder notation for a line, and

00:37:10.480 --> 00:37:13.420
we will see this for a plane and
other things shortly,

00:37:13.420 --> 00:37:16.420
is called the parametric

00:37:27.340 --> 00:37:31.200
form, where the parameter that
we are using here is a.

00:37:33.640 --> 00:37:38.460
But sometimes the parameter we
will use is something like T or

00:37:38.460 --> 00:37:39.200
S.

00:37:39.200 --> 00:37:43.700
We'll see some examples of that
at the moment.

00:37:50.060 --> 00:37:51.060
OK,

00:37:51.200 --> 00:37:52.680
so what are some of the big
ideas here?

00:37:52.680 --> 00:37:56.800
If I have a line in R2 that goes
through the origin,

00:37:56.800 --> 00:37:59.120
I know it is the span of a
vector.

00:37:59.120 --> 00:38:02.320
This line is not passing through
the origin,

00:38:02.320 --> 00:38:04.580
so it is not the span of a
vector.

00:38:04.580 --> 00:38:08.800
But we can still express it as
an offset plus the span of a

00:38:08.800 --> 00:38:09.800
vector.

00:38:11.440 --> 00:38:16.780
And that's why there is this
extra vector up here.

00:38:18.140 --> 00:38:20.620
So it's not the span of a
vector,

00:38:20.620 --> 00:38:25.120
but it's like a shifted version
of the span of a vector.

00:38:26.380 --> 00:38:28.580
What questions do we have so
far?

00:38:28.580 --> 00:38:29.580
Awesome.

00:38:32.580 --> 00:38:37.640
Is it still in terms of just the
span of one vector even?

00:38:37.640 --> 00:38:39.760
Correct, correct.
Because I'm not multiplying that

00:38:39.760 --> 00:38:41.480
0, 6 by anything.

00:38:41.480 --> 00:38:41.940
Good.

00:38:41.940 --> 00:38:43.080
So that's important.

00:38:43.080 --> 00:38:45.400
In a little bit, we will
multiply a second vector by

00:38:45.400 --> 00:38:48.300
something.
But the shape of that will be

00:38:48.300 --> 00:38:49.240
different.

00:38:49.240 --> 00:38:50.240
Good.

00:38:52.140 --> 00:38:56.420
And I'll say this is the offset.

00:38:56.420 --> 00:38:59.700
And if you want to think of the
role of 3-12 here,

00:38:59.700 --> 00:39:04.200
you can think of it as telling
us to start there first and then

00:39:04.200 --> 00:39:07.200
slide along the line.

00:39:08.340 --> 00:39:10.800
One more definition, just
because it'll show up from time

00:39:10.800 --> 00:39:11.900
to time.

00:39:11.900 --> 00:39:16.400
This line is what's called an
affine line,

00:39:17.720 --> 00:39:21.520
and all that is is just a line
that doesn't have to contain 0,

00:39:21.520 --> 00:39:22.520
0.

00:39:37.900 --> 00:39:41.180
The type of exercise that you
might see in a lab or homework

00:39:41.180 --> 00:39:46.700
is using the parametric form of
a line, convert it to y equals

00:39:46.700 --> 00:39:49.700
mx plus b form.

00:39:49.840 --> 00:39:51.940
And what are some ways you could
do that?

00:39:51.940 --> 00:39:52.360
Well,

00:39:52.360 --> 00:39:55.560
the direction vector tells you
what the slope is.

00:39:55.560 --> 00:39:57.960
It's the y value over the x
value.

00:39:57.960 --> 00:40:01.500
And then the position vector at
the start tells you a point on

00:40:01.500 --> 00:40:03.000
it.
So you can use tools that you

00:40:03.000 --> 00:40:05.340
knew about before to solve for
what that would be.

00:40:05.340 --> 00:40:07.480
If you didn't think of that, you
can just plug in two different

00:40:07.480 --> 00:40:08.660
values for a.

00:40:08.660 --> 00:40:09.880
And that'll give you two points
on the line.

00:40:09.880 --> 00:40:12.240
And then you can use tools you
knew about before to find the

00:40:12.240 --> 00:40:15.500
equation of a line from two
points.

00:40:15.500 --> 00:40:18.420
Now, what about if we move to
R3?

00:40:18.420 --> 00:40:19.200
So remember,

00:40:19.200 --> 00:40:21.740
we're still thinking of just one
vector,

00:40:21.740 --> 00:40:23.990
but in R3.

00:40:26.060 --> 00:40:29.000
So now we're imagining this
vector living in

00:40:29.000 --> 00:40:30.460
three-dimensional space,

00:40:30.460 --> 00:40:34.960
and let's consider the vector v
equals, I don't know, 252.

00:40:35.940 --> 00:40:38.940
What is its span?

00:40:45.280 --> 00:40:46.000
Yeah.

00:40:46.000 --> 00:40:47.980
Is it still just a line in R3?

00:40:47.980 --> 00:40:48.540
Yeah,

00:40:48.540 --> 00:40:50.760
it's a line in R3.

00:40:50.760 --> 00:40:52.260
Those exist.

00:40:56.780 --> 00:41:01.280
And it would look something like
this.

00:41:02.900 --> 00:41:08.700
And here I might call this the
parametric form

00:41:08.700 --> 00:41:12.960
of a line in R3.

00:41:14.920 --> 00:41:19.500
Okay, now one of the last things
I said on the previous page was

00:41:19.500 --> 00:41:21.400
that this line in R2,

00:41:21.400 --> 00:41:22.240
the one in red,

00:41:22.240 --> 00:41:24.740
we've described it in parametric
form,

00:41:24.740 --> 00:41:29.280
but you could figure out how to
write this line in y equals mx

00:41:29.280 --> 00:41:30.460
plus b form.

00:41:30.460 --> 00:41:32.320
It's a line in 2d.

00:41:32.320 --> 00:41:34.520
What about this line?

00:41:34.520 --> 00:41:40.000
A line in 3d and because it's
the span of a vector it passes

00:41:40.000 --> 00:41:41.160
through the origin.

00:41:41.160 --> 00:41:46.410
Can this be written as a linear
equation?

00:41:59.160 --> 00:42:00.920
A couple of things I want to
show you here while you think

00:42:00.920 --> 00:42:03.000
about the answer to that.

00:42:03.000 --> 00:42:08.640
Can this line be written in the

00:42:08.640 --> 00:42:12.800
form y equals mx plus b? Well,
here, there would be a third

00:42:12.800 --> 00:42:16.240
direction, so we would have a z.

00:42:16.240 --> 00:42:21.490
Can this be written as z equals
ax plus by?

00:42:26.240 --> 00:42:28.490
I don't know.
When in doubt,

00:42:29.660 --> 00:42:34.160
why don't we go to Desmos?

00:42:35.100 --> 00:42:38.100
Maybe it'll tell us.

00:42:40.940 --> 00:42:45.360
Okay, So my theory is, OK, let's
see what z equals

00:42:45.360 --> 00:42:51.180
something times x plus something
times

00:42:51.180 --> 00:42:53.430
y gives us.

00:42:53.860 --> 00:42:56.300
2x minus 3y.

00:42:57.460 --> 00:43:00.460
Is that a line?

00:43:00.620 --> 00:43:02.560
No, what is it?

00:43:02.560 --> 00:43:04.810
It's a plane.

00:43:05.300 --> 00:43:08.640
So using that understanding, is
it possible to write an equation

00:43:08.640 --> 00:43:13.860
in terms of x, y, and z that
describes a line in 3D.

00:43:13.860 --> 00:43:14.860
Why?

00:43:15.020 --> 00:43:18.020
It is not possible.
Why is it not possible?

00:43:19.080 --> 00:43:24.760
I think it's because the v would
be dependent on one specific

00:43:24.760 --> 00:43:25.760
variable.

00:43:26.220 --> 00:43:27.080
Exactly.

00:43:27.080 --> 00:43:30.190
The tricky thing with a line in
3D

00:43:30.190 --> 00:43:32.450
is that while it exists in 3D,

00:43:32.450 --> 00:43:35.860
it only moves in one direction.
So there's really only one

00:43:35.860 --> 00:43:40.360
parameter that you can use to
control your position on it.

00:43:40.600 --> 00:43:42.400
Here's another way of thinking
about it.

00:43:42.400 --> 00:43:48.340
If there is a function f of xy
that described a

00:43:48.340 --> 00:43:48.860
line,

00:43:48.860 --> 00:43:52.200
it would mean that I can think
of any x and any y, and it would

00:43:52.200 --> 00:43:54.040
tell me a place on the line.

00:43:54.040 --> 00:43:54.880
But a line,

00:43:54.880 --> 00:43:57.980
imagine I draw a line in this
room, like from my arm to the

00:43:57.980 --> 00:44:00.020
exit side over there.

00:44:00.020 --> 00:44:03.140
Not every point in the room is
under the line.

00:44:03.140 --> 00:44:06.100
Only one very specific
combination of x's and y's lives

00:44:06.100 --> 00:44:07.080
in this line.

00:44:07.080 --> 00:44:10.720
So it's not the case that if all
of you took the coordinates that

00:44:10.720 --> 00:44:11.360
you were at,

00:44:11.360 --> 00:44:13.500
like what row, comma, what
column you're at,

00:44:13.500 --> 00:44:15.740
that the line passes above you.
It doesn't.

00:44:15.740 --> 00:44:17.780
A plane would, though.
If we just took a plane,

00:44:17.780 --> 00:44:20.030
remember planes extend
infinitely in all directions,

00:44:20.060 --> 00:44:22.440
if I covered the room with the
