01:00:41.160 --> 01:00:43.540
This is the kind of thing you
need to convince yourself of.

01:00:43.540 --> 01:00:45.680
Just make up two vectors in R2,

01:00:45.680 --> 01:00:48.020
then make up a third one, and
just try and make the third one

01:00:48.020 --> 01:00:50.220
using a linear combination of
the first two.

01:00:50.220 --> 01:00:52.480
And as long as the first two
aren't scalar multiples of each

01:00:52.480 --> 01:00:55.120
other, you will be able to do
it.

01:00:55.120 --> 01:00:57.900
So that's two vectors in R2.

01:00:57.900 --> 01:00:59.740
What about two vectors in R3?

01:00:59.740 --> 01:01:02.740
What are the possibilities?

01:01:18.340 --> 01:01:20.840
Same three possibilities to
consider.

01:01:20.840 --> 01:01:22.860
Their span could be the zero
vector,

01:01:22.860 --> 01:01:26.140
their span could be a line, or
their span could be what is the

01:01:26.140 --> 01:01:27.640
third thing?

01:01:34.440 --> 01:01:35.440
Yeah.

01:01:35.700 --> 01:01:37.200
A plane.

01:01:49.160 --> 01:01:52.920
I don't know why this guy is
just rotating freely.

01:01:52.920 --> 01:01:53.920
Good.

01:01:56.860 --> 01:01:57.860
Good.

01:02:00.580 --> 01:02:04.400
There's a bit more intuition for
some of these things in the

01:02:04.400 --> 01:02:05.300
notes.

01:02:05.300 --> 01:02:05.940
Like,

01:02:05.940 --> 01:02:09.120
what would happen if you had two
vectors in R2

01:02:09.120 --> 01:02:11.560
that are scalar multiples of
each other? What would happen

01:02:11.560 --> 01:02:16.380
from the equation perspective if
you tried to find a third vector

01:02:16.380 --> 01:02:19.100
as a linear combination of the
first two? Here's a good example

01:02:19.100 --> 01:02:20.780
of where you wouldn't be able to
do that.

01:02:20.780 --> 01:02:22.600
Here this is asking,

01:02:22.600 --> 01:02:26.980
can I use the orange vector 1 1
and the blue vector 2 2 to make

01:02:26.980 --> 01:02:28.280
8 11?

01:02:28.280 --> 01:02:31.380
You can't do it because any
linear combination of 1 1 2 2 is

01:02:31.380 --> 01:02:34.280
gonna have both of its
components equal and 8 11

01:02:34.280 --> 01:02:36.220
doesn't have both of its
components equal.

01:02:36.220 --> 01:02:38.440
So you didn't like if you try
and solve there will be no

01:02:38.440 --> 01:02:41.040
solution for a 1 and a 2.
It's the kind of thing that you

01:02:41.040 --> 01:02:43.720
will review in homework 4.

01:02:43.720 --> 01:02:47.000
But anyways, now we're looking
at two vectors in R3.

01:02:47.000 --> 01:02:49.060
my orange vector and my blue
vector,

01:02:49.060 --> 01:02:51.020
and what you're seeing in gray
are a few of their linear

01:02:51.020 --> 01:02:52.020
combinations.

01:02:52.500 --> 01:02:57.000
What you should notice is that
they all live on this same flat

01:02:57.000 --> 01:02:58.000
plane.

01:02:58.660 --> 01:03:04.100
You can imagine drawing a sheet
of paper in 3D in which all of

01:03:04.100 --> 01:03:05.080
these vectors live,

01:03:05.080 --> 01:03:07.500
and all of the linear
combinations of v1 and v2 here.

01:03:07.500 --> 01:03:10.620
I should zoom in a little more
here to make this clear, But all

01:03:10.620 --> 01:03:13.320
of these linear combinations of
v1 and v2 live on this plane,

01:03:13.320 --> 01:03:14.320
okay?

01:03:15.480 --> 01:03:18.740
So if a third vector that I
present you with is on that

01:03:18.740 --> 01:03:21.340
plane Well, then you can make it
with v1 and v2.

01:03:21.340 --> 01:03:24.020
If it's not, like if I give you
a third vector

01:03:24.020 --> 01:03:25.700
That's poking somewhere out of
