01:03:25.700 --> 01:03:29.380
Well, then you can't make it
with v1 and v2

01:03:29.380 --> 01:03:33.130
Okay, and that plane is
visualized here

01:03:35.200 --> 01:03:36.200
Okay

01:03:37.260 --> 01:03:38.760
Let me

01:03:40.000 --> 01:03:44.140
Take a screenshot of this and
put this in the iPad notes.

01:03:44.140 --> 01:03:48.640
What questions do we have about
the fact

01:03:50.280 --> 01:03:56.200
that two vectors in R3 span a
plane as long as those

01:03:56.200 --> 01:04:00.700
vectors don't point in the same
direction?

01:04:01.960 --> 01:04:04.800
Are we comfortable with that
idea?

01:04:04.800 --> 01:04:08.500
Now, how can you describe that
plane?

01:04:08.500 --> 01:04:10.480
there are two ways you can do
it.

01:04:10.480 --> 01:04:13.540
You can describe it in
parametric form,

01:04:13.540 --> 01:04:19.360
which would be something like
the set A

01:04:19.360 --> 01:04:20.860
times V1,

01:04:21.160 --> 01:04:24.360
whatever V1 is, plus B times V2,

01:04:24.360 --> 01:04:28.520
where A and B are real numbers.

01:04:28.520 --> 01:04:33.020
Or you could describe that plane
as a linear equation.

01:04:33.980 --> 01:04:37.920
On Desmos, 10 minutes ago, we
discovered that equations of the

01:04:37.920 --> 01:04:42.680
form z equals ax plus by
describe planes in R3.

01:04:43.780 --> 01:04:46.400
So there is a way to find a
linear equation for a plane in

01:04:46.400 --> 01:04:50.920
R3 given the two vectors, given
two vectors that span it.

01:04:55.480 --> 01:05:01.320
Another form instead of z equals
ax plus by is ax plus By

01:05:01.320 --> 01:05:03.740
plus Cz equals 0.

01:05:03.740 --> 01:05:08.940
And when you see chapter 4.4,
which talks more about how you

01:05:08.940 --> 01:05:10.380
write it in this form,

01:05:10.380 --> 01:05:13.040
you will see why we prefer this
form.

01:05:13.040 --> 01:05:16.420
And the short answer is that if
you look at this equation, Ax

01:05:16.420 --> 01:05:19.620
plus By plus Cz equals 0,

01:05:19.620 --> 01:05:24.160
if you consider all of the x,
y's and z's that satisfy this,

01:05:24.160 --> 01:05:27.160
they're really just describing
the set of vectors that are

01:05:27.160 --> 01:05:29.520
orthogonal to the vector ABC.

01:05:29.520 --> 01:05:34.020
Note that this just looks like
the dot product of XYZ with ABC,

01:05:34.120 --> 01:05:36.320
and it's saying this plane is
all of the vectors that are

01:05:36.320 --> 01:05:38.570
orthogonal to ABC.

01:05:39.460 --> 01:05:42.500
And the way we'll think about
it, and this was actually the

01:05:42.500 --> 01:05:44.580
last question in yesterday's
lab, which I'm pretty sure you

01:05:44.580 --> 01:05:45.300
didn't get to,

01:05:45.300 --> 01:05:48.940
but this was the idea,

01:05:48.940 --> 01:05:53.460
if you consider a vector n that
is orthogonal to the plane, if

01:05:53.460 --> 01:05:54.960
you find that,

01:05:54.960 --> 01:05:59.380
then the components of n are the
coefficients of x, y, and z in

01:05:59.380 --> 01:06:00.880
this equation.

01:06:01.220 --> 01:06:03.660
And again, you will practice
with this in the upcoming weeks.

01:06:03.660 --> 01:06:06.520
I'm not expecting you to
automatically know how to do

01:06:06.520 --> 01:06:07.520
that.

01:06:08.580 --> 01:06:12.740
So that's the situation with two
vectors in R3.

01:06:12.740 --> 01:06:17.240
What about two vectors in a
space that we can't visualize,

01:06:18.300 --> 01:06:19.300
R7?

01:06:21.100 --> 01:06:23.760
Well they could span the zero
vector, they could span a line

01:06:23.760 --> 01:06:29.010
in R7, or they could span a
plane in R7.

01:06:30.900 --> 01:06:35.360
But what was the new word that I
introduced to describe slices of

01:06:35.360 --> 01:06:36.360
space?

01:06:54.620 --> 01:06:57.740
Using that new word, what would
I describe the span of these two

01:06:57.740 --> 01:06:58.740
vectors?

01:06:59.340 --> 01:06:59.600
Yeah,

01:06:59.600 --> 01:07:00.280
Sam.

01:07:00.280 --> 01:07:01.120
Subspace?

01:07:01.120 --> 01:07:05.620
Yeah, I would call it a
two-dimensional subspace of R7.

01:07:06.380 --> 01:07:09.040
So another question that someone
asked in the Google form was,

01:07:09.040 --> 01:07:12.300
is a subspace, is that word
unique to lines?

01:07:12.300 --> 01:07:12.960
No.

01:07:12.960 --> 01:07:16.860
It's unique to all slices, which
you should think of as flat

01:07:16.860 --> 01:07:18.660
objects in high dimensions.

01:07:18.660 --> 01:07:20.960
Just like a line is flat, it
doesn't have any curves.

01:07:20.960 --> 01:07:23.320
A plane is flat, it doesn't have
any curves.

01:07:23.320 --> 01:07:25.280
Whatever the span of these two
vectors is,

01:07:25.280 --> 01:07:26.960
while we can't visualize it,

01:07:26.960 --> 01:07:29.340
hopefully it's not too hard to
believe that it doesn't have any

01:07:29.340 --> 01:07:30.380
bends or curves.

01:07:30.380 --> 01:07:33.340
Whatever it is, it's flat
wherever it lives.

01:07:33.340 --> 01:07:38.590
And that comes from just the way
that we add vectors, tip to tip.

01:07:39.240 --> 01:07:43.740
So the span of these two
vectors, v1 and v2,

01:07:44.340 --> 01:07:45.840
which, again,

01:07:46.060 --> 01:07:49.810
I would write in parametric
form, maybe like this,

01:07:50.440 --> 01:07:53.160
a1 times this thing

01:07:53.160 --> 01:08:02.300
plus

01:08:02.300 --> 01:08:04.360
a2.
It doesn't really matter again

01:08:04.360 --> 01:08:07.920
if you use A1 and A2 or AB here,
we only have two, so I could

01:08:07.920 --> 01:08:11.280
have used A and B.

01:08:11.280 --> 01:08:13.530
It's this set

01:08:14.980 --> 01:08:18.780
where I loop over all possible
values of A1 and A2,

01:08:18.780 --> 01:08:24.030
and what I would say is that
this is a two-dimensional

01:08:26.540 --> 01:08:28.790
subspace of R7.

01:08:29.420 --> 01:08:31.780
And remember, a subspace,

01:08:31.780 --> 01:08:35.360
I want you to think of
intuitively as a slice.

01:08:35.360 --> 01:08:39.110
I'll give you a formal
definition on Tuesday.

01:08:41.400 --> 01:08:42.400
OK.

01:08:42.940 --> 01:08:46.140
So we have some understanding
that if I have two vectors,

01:08:46.140 --> 01:08:50.000
whether they're an R2, R3, or
R7, or wherever,

01:08:50.000 --> 01:08:52.120
their span could be just the
zero vector.

01:08:52.120 --> 01:08:55.160
It could be just a line if they
point in the same direction.

01:08:55.160 --> 01:08:58.560
Or it could be a two-dimensional
subspace of whatever universe

01:08:58.560 --> 01:09:01.620
they live in.
A two-dimensional subspace of R2

01:09:01.620 --> 01:09:05.380
is like a two-dimensional slice
of a thing that only has two

01:09:05.380 --> 01:09:06.480
dimensions.
So it's a whole thing.

01:09:06.600 --> 01:09:08.180
It's all of R2.

01:09:08.180 --> 01:09:10.840
A two-dimensional slice of R3,
that's a plane,

01:09:10.840 --> 01:09:13.840
which we saw several examples
of.

01:09:13.840 --> 01:09:16.160
A two-dimensional slice of R7,

01:09:16.160 --> 01:09:19.080
hard to think about what that
means, but take this as a

01:09:19.080 --> 01:09:20.020
definition for now.

01:09:20.020 --> 01:09:22.580
It's like a slice of
seven-dimensional space in which

01:09:22.580 --> 01:09:24.120
I can only move in two
directions.

01:09:24.120 --> 01:09:28.040
The directions in which I can
move are defined by these two

01:09:28.040 --> 01:09:29.400
spanning vectors,

01:09:29.400 --> 01:09:34.650
and the amounts by which I can
move are a1 and a2 here.

01:09:38.460 --> 01:09:41.540
Okay, one thing I want to
emphasize before we move to the

01:09:41.540 --> 01:09:44.460
three vector case and more
general,

01:09:44.460 --> 01:09:48.980
here I've written this plane in
parametric form as a times v1

01:09:48.980 --> 01:09:51.980
plus b times v2.

01:09:52.580 --> 01:09:57.220
Are those the only two vectors
that define this plane, or could

01:09:57.220 --> 01:09:59.920
I have defined this plane in
parametric form using other

01:09:59.920 --> 01:10:00.920
vectors?

01:10:02.880 --> 01:10:04.880
Could I do it with other
vectors, or is there something

01:10:04.880 --> 01:10:06.620
special about this v1 and v2?

01:10:06.620 --> 01:10:07.860
Yeah.
You could do scalar multiples,

01:10:08.100 --> 01:10:08.360
right?

01:10:08.360 --> 01:10:10.820
I could do scalar multiples, or
I could do just other vectors on

01:10:10.820 --> 01:10:11.580
the plane.

01:10:11.580 --> 01:10:14.380
Any two vectors that live on
this plane, they don't need to

01:10:14.380 --> 01:10:16.960
be scalar multiples of v1 and v2
specifically.

01:10:16.960 --> 01:10:20.000
I can pick any two vectors that
live in this plane as long as

01:10:20.000 --> 01:10:23.560
they don't point in the same
direction they span this plane

01:10:23.560 --> 01:10:27.800
Okay, so I could have picked

01:10:27.800 --> 01:10:32.300
You know call that v3 and

01:10:32.400 --> 01:10:36.900
Call that v4 that's a terrible
color to use here

01:10:41.710 --> 01:10:42.710
OK,

01:10:42.750 --> 01:10:48.750
the span of v1 and v2 is also
equal to the span of v3 and v4.

01:10:54.450 --> 01:10:57.590
Let's move this over here.

01:10:57.590 --> 01:11:00.510
Do we see why that's the case?

01:11:00.510 --> 01:11:04.350
I picked two vectors that are
not pointing in the same

01:11:04.350 --> 01:11:06.390
direction that live in the
plane.

01:11:06.390 --> 01:11:07.390
Yeah, Emma.

01:11:07.390 --> 01:11:11.810
Just to clarify, when you say
that two vectors pointing in the

01:11:11.810 --> 01:11:12.910
same direction,

01:11:12.910 --> 01:11:15.590
do you mean that they're equal?

01:11:15.590 --> 01:11:18.170
Well, first to clarify here, I
meant I picked two vectors that

01:11:18.170 --> 01:11:19.570
don't point in the same
direction.

01:11:19.570 --> 01:11:22.790
But when I say point in the same
direction, I mean are scalar

01:11:22.790 --> 01:11:24.230
multiples of each other,

01:11:24.230 --> 01:11:25.910
like 1, 1 and 2, 2, for example.

01:11:25.910 --> 01:11:29.190
Not equal, but they have the
same span.

01:11:29.190 --> 01:11:29.970
Good.

01:11:29.970 --> 01:11:31.470
Good clarification.

01:11:36.330 --> 01:11:38.790
I don't think these colors all
need to be accurate, but

01:11:38.790 --> 01:11:40.650
whatever, it's OK.

01:11:40.650 --> 01:11:42.290
Forces me to go slower.

01:11:42.290 --> 01:11:45.550
OK, are we clear on why the span
of v1 and v2 here is the same as

01:11:45.550 --> 01:11:48.090
the span of v3 and v4?

01:11:48.090 --> 01:11:49.090
OK.

01:11:49.550 --> 01:11:53.230
And these are all just random
choices on this plane.

01:11:53.230 --> 01:11:55.130
We could all think of two
different vectors that live on

01:11:55.130 --> 01:11:56.950
the plane.
And this plane is the span of

01:11:56.950 --> 01:11:58.950
those two vectors.

01:11:58.950 --> 01:11:59.950
OK,

01:12:00.250 --> 01:12:01.810
so we've looked at the span of
one vector.

01:12:01.810 --> 01:12:02.890
We've looked at the span of two
vectors.

01:12:02.890 --> 01:12:05.750
Let's think about three vectors

01:12:05.750 --> 01:12:08.590
We'll start with R2 then think
about R3 then think about in

01:12:08.590 --> 01:12:11.090
general and I'm hoping we can
get to the like main takeaway in

01:12:11.090 --> 01:12:13.930
general by the end of today

01:12:13.930 --> 01:12:17.680
Okay, what about three vectors
in R2?

01:12:17.990 --> 01:12:20.810
What could they span

01:12:20.810 --> 01:12:24.750
They could span the zero vector
sure they could span a line if I

01:12:24.750 --> 01:12:27.870
just give you three vectors that
already live on the same line

01:12:27.870 --> 01:12:31.470
They could span all of R2

01:12:31.470 --> 01:12:34.410
Because we know that you only
need two vectors to span all of

01:12:34.410 --> 01:12:38.310
r2, right? If I give you just
two building blocks in r2

01:12:38.310 --> 01:12:40.050
That don't point in the same
direction.

01:12:40.050 --> 01:12:42.770
I know they already span all of
r2

01:12:42.770 --> 01:12:47.270
So what does having a third
building block enable in r2?

01:12:52.450 --> 01:12:53.450
Yeah

01:12:53.970 --> 01:12:54.970
Nothing,

01:12:55.010 --> 01:12:57.290
the third one is kind of just
there to stir stuff up.

01:12:57.290 --> 01:13:00.970
Okay, it doesn't allow you to
make anything that two vectors

01:13:00.970 --> 01:13:02.470
couldn't already,

01:13:02.610 --> 01:13:03.510
okay?

01:13:03.510 --> 01:13:05.950
The span of three vectors in R2,

01:13:05.950 --> 01:13:09.590
the most it could be is still
just all of R2, okay?

01:13:09.590 --> 01:13:11.650
If I'm drawing vectors on my
page,

01:13:11.650 --> 01:13:13.410
doesn't matter how many there
are, they can't somehow

01:13:13.410 --> 01:13:15.130
magically jump out of my iPad,

01:13:15.130 --> 01:13:17.530
right? They're stuck to live in
R2,

01:13:17.530 --> 01:13:18.210
okay?

01:13:18.210 --> 01:13:20.170
So here I've drawn

01:13:20.170 --> 01:13:24.710
two configurations of three
vectors in R2 that are subtly

01:13:24.710 --> 01:13:26.090
different.
On the right, I have three

01:13:26.090 --> 01:13:31.340
vectors in R2, and none of them
point in the same direction.

01:13:31.370 --> 01:13:32.870
But still,

01:13:33.490 --> 01:13:35.110
one of them is redundant.

01:13:35.110 --> 01:13:40.290
I could delete this pink vector
